# CastAdhan Portable — Tailscale auto-enrolment configuration
# ============================================================
# Copy this file to one of the locations `setup-pi.sh` checks
# (in priority order) BEFORE running the installer:
#
#   /etc/default/castadhan-tailscale        — on the Pi, mode 0600, root-owned
#   /boot/castadhan-tailscale.env           — on the SD card boot partition
#                                              (Pi OS Bookworm: /boot/firmware/)
#
# Either path works. The /boot/ option is the easiest for "flash a gift Pi
# and forget" workflows because you can drop the file onto the SD card from
# your Mac/Windows machine BEFORE first boot — the Pi picks it up the first
# time setup-pi.sh runs, enrols on your tailnet, then SHREDS the file so the
# key can't be recovered from the SD card later.
#
# Then either:
#   - Use a one-shot, pre-approved Tailscale auth key (safest — single use,
#     auto-revokes after enrolment). Generate at:
#       https://login.tailscale.com/admin/settings/keys
#     One-shot key flow: tick "Reusable: OFF", "Ephemeral: OFF",
#     "Pre-approved: ON", expiration 7-30 days.
#
#   - Or a reusable key if you're flashing many Pis from one batch (less
#     secure — if the SD card leaks, the key can enrol arbitrary devices to
#     your tailnet until revoked).
#
# DO NOT commit this file with a real auth key into git. The .gitignore
# explicitly excludes the live filename `castadhan-tailscale.defaults` and
# the boot variant `castadhan-tailscale.env`. Only the .template file
# (this one) belongs in version control.

# ---- the auth key (REQUIRED) -----------------------------------------------
# Format: tskey-auth-XXXXXXXXX-YYYYYYYYY (45-60 chars)
# Generate at https://login.tailscale.com/admin/settings/keys
TS_AUTHKEY=""

# ---- the hostname the Pi will register as on your tailnet (REQUIRED) -------
# Recommend something descriptive: "aunt-pi-ghent", "son-pi-haverfordwest",
# "neighbour-pi-london". Appears in your Tailscale Machines page.
# Default is the Pi's own hostname (typically "castadhan"), but for gift Pis
# you want something distinctive so you can tell them apart.
TS_HOSTNAME=""

# ---- extra tailscale up flags (OPTIONAL) -----------------------------------
# Defaults to "--ssh" which enables Tailscale's built-in SSH server. Other
# useful flags:
#   --accept-routes        : receive routes advertised by other tailnet nodes
#   --advertise-tags=tag:gift-pi   : tag this Pi for ACL grouping (requires
#                                    that you've defined the tag in your ACL)
#   --shields-up           : block incoming connections except SSH (recommended
#                            for gift Pis — operator still SSH-reachable,
#                            household can't accidentally expose it)
# TS_EXTRA_ARGS="--ssh --shields-up"
